"use strict";(self.webpackChunkctaceygreen_github_io=self.webpackChunkctaceygreen_github_io||[]).push([[1477],{10:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"blogPosts":[{"id":"your-engineers-are-ai-native-is-your-organisation","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/your-engineers-are-ai-native-is-your-organisation","editUrl":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen/ctaceygreen.github.io/blog/2026-11-06-ai-native-organisation/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-06-ai-native-organisation/index.md","title":"Your Engineers Are AI-Native. Is Your Organisation?","description":"Engineering Fundamentals in the Age of AI - Part 1 - Organising for Flow","date":"2026-11-06T00:00:00.000Z","formattedDate":"November 6, 2026","tags":[{"label":"AI","permalink":"/blog/tags/ai"},{"label":"team-topologies","permalink":"/blog/tags/team-topologies"},{"label":"engineering-fundamentals","permalink":"/blog/tags/engineering-fundamentals"},{"label":"platform-engineering","permalink":"/blog/tags/platform-engineering"},{"label":"devops","permalink":"/blog/tags/devops"},{"label":"leadership","permalink":"/blog/tags/leadership"}],"readingTime":34.55,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Chris Tacey-Green","title":"Engineer, Architect, Human","url":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen","image_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"your-engineers-are-ai-native-is-your-organisation","title":"Your Engineers Are AI-Native. Is Your Organisation?","description":"Engineering Fundamentals in the Age of AI - Part 1 - Organising for Flow","authors":[{"name":"Chris Tacey-Green","title":"Engineer, Architect, Human","url":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen","image_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4"}],"image":"https://christaceygreen.com/2026-11-06-ai-native-organisation/title-image.png","tags":["AI","team-topologies","engineering-fundamentals","platform-engineering","devops","leadership"]},"nextItem":{"title":"Attention (to detail) is all you need","permalink":"/blog/attention-to-detail-is-all-you-need"}},"content":":::info\\n*Engineering Fundamentals in the Age of AI \u2014 Part 1: Organising for Flow*\\n:::\\n\\nI\'ve been spending a lot of time recently thinking about what an AI-native software engineering organisation should look like.\\n\\nFirstly, some context. I\'m all-in on AI engineering tooling. Claude Code, Codex, and the growing ecosystem of agentic development tools are incredible. The progress we\'ve made in such a short space of time is staggering, and I\'m convinced we\'re only scratching the surface of what\'s possible.\\n\\nI\'ve seen just how much these tools amplify great engineers. We\'re moving from engineers writing code, to engineers orchestrating multiple agents that are writing, testing, debugging and improving software simultaneously. An engineer running 5-10 agents at once is becoming increasingly realistic, and the amount of work that a capable engineer can get through is quite remarkable.\\n\\nI\'m not going to spend this post convincing you of that. There are plenty of people writing about how to get the most out of Claude Code, how to structure your agents, how to optimise your prompts, and which model is best at writing TypeScript this week.\\n\\nWhat I want to talk about is something I think unlocks a lot more value.\\n\\nWe\'re spending enormous amounts of time and energy making our *engineers* AI-native.\\n\\n**But are we making our *organisations* AI-native?**\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\nImagine you\'ve got an engineer who can now implement a feature in a couple of hours. Fantastic! Their agents have written the code, created tests, run them, fixed the failures, and everything looks good.\\n\\n***Now what?***\\n\\n- Perhaps the change needs to go to a separate QA team for regression testing.\\n- Maybe an architect needs to review it.\\n- Perhaps there\'s a release process involving another team, and we\'ve missed this week\'s release window.\\n\\nAnd when it finally reaches customers, we discover that the original requirements weren\'t quite right anyway, because the people building the feature weren\'t involved in the months of discovery that happened before it landed with engineering.\\n\\nWe\'ve made writing the software incredibly fast. **The customer is still waiting weeks for something useful.**\\n\\nI\'m exaggerating, of course. But not by much.\\n\\nWorse still, we\'ve potentially increased the amount of work arriving at all those downstream bottlenecks. Our supercharged engineers are producing changes faster than the organisation can actually deliver them. And no I\'m not talking about code review bottlenecks here, that\'s being discussed enough in seemingly every forum I stumble upon (maybe a topic for another day).\\n\\n:::info\\n**Making your engineers AI-native doesn\'t automatically make your software delivery organisation AI-native.**\\n:::\\n\\nAnd I think this is where a lot of organisations are going to struggle to realise the full potential of AI.\\n\\n## The fundamentals haven\'t suddenly stopped mattering\\n\\nSomething I\'ve been thinking about is how much of what we need to do to become truly AI-native is actually new.\\n\\nWe talk about AI as though it\'s requiring us to completely reinvent software engineering. And yes, there are some genuinely fundamental changes happening in how we build software.\\n\\nBut when you look at what an effective AI-native delivery organisation needs, you\'ll keep coming back to things we\'ve been advocating for years.\\n\\n- Small, empowered teams.\\n- Clear ownership boundaries.\\n- Engineers close to customers.\\n- Automated testing.\\n- Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery.\\n- DevOps practices.\\n- Self-service platforms.\\n- Fast feedback loops.\\n\\nWe\'ve been talking about all this stuff for ages!\\n\\nBut plenty of organisations haven\'t got these fundamentals setup yet.\\n\\nThe [2025 DORA research](https://dora.dev/research/2025/dora-report/) describes AI as an **amplifier** of an organisation\'s existing strengths and weaknesses. I think by now we\'ve all seen this in real life. If you\'ve got great engineering practices, strong teams and a delivery process optimised for flow, AI can massively amplify what you\'re capable of achieving.\\n\\nBut if your delivery process is full of handoffs, manual steps, disconnected teams and slow decision-making, AI isn\'t going to magically solve those problems.\\n\\n**It will likely make them worse.**\\n\\nThis has led me to want to explore some of the engineering fundamentals that I think are becoming even more important as we move into the age of AI.\\n\\nNot because they\'re new, but because the impact of getting them right, or wrong, is becoming considerably greater.\\n\\nOver a series of posts, I want to get into things like DevOps, CI/CD, quality engineering, platform engineering, and how we adapt our delivery practices when engineers are working alongside a greater and greater number of autonomous agents.\\n\\nBut first let\'s start with something more fundamental.\\n\\n***How should we structure our teams and organisations to get the most value from AI?***\\n\\nAnd for me, the answer starts with Team Topologies.\\n\\n## Team Topologies. Again?!\\n\\nThose who\'ve worked with me will know I\'m a big fan of [Team Topologies](https://teamtopologies.com/key-concepts/), by Matthew Skelton and Manuel Pais. I\'ve been advocating for and applying its principles for years.\\n\\nIf you\'ve not come across it before, it\'s worth spending a little time explaining what it is, because it\'s pretty central to everything I\'m going to talk about.\\n\\nTeam Topologies is an approach to organising software delivery teams around the ***fast flow of value***.\\n\\n***What does that actually mean?***\\n\\nImagine a customer has a problem. Perhaps there\'s something frustrating about the product they\'re using, or a new capability that would make their life considerably easier.\\n\\nHow long does it take for that problem to become a working solution in their hands?\\n\\nAnd, importantly, what\'s happening during that time?\\n\\nAre people actually solving the problem? Or is work sitting in queues, waiting for somebody from another team to pick it up, review it, approve it or pass it somewhere else?\\n\\nTeam Topologies encourages us to organise teams so that they can deliver value with as little unnecessary friction as possible.\\n\\nIt describes four fundamental team types.\\n\\n- **Stream-aligned teams** are the ones delivering value within a clearly defined business or customer domain. They should be able to understand a problem, develop a solution, deliver it and operate it, without constantly depending on other teams.\\n- **Enabling teams** are groups of specialists who help stream-aligned teams develop capabilities they might be missing. Perhaps a team needs to improve its testing practices, understand a new architectural approach or develop better security capabilities. An enabling team helps it become more capable, rather than permanently taking over part of its delivery process.\\n- **Platform teams** provide shared capabilities that other teams can consume with minimal friction. That might include deployment infrastructure, observability tooling, development environments or other services that abstract away complexity. The idea is that our stream-aligned teams can focus on the problems that matter to their customers, rather than repeatedly solving the same infrastructure and engineering challenges.\\n- **Complicated-subsystem teams** own parts of a system that genuinely require deep specialist expertise. Perhaps there\'s an unusually complex algorithm, a specialist machine learning capability or some other technical challenge that would be unreasonable for every stream-aligned team to understand.\\n\\n![Diagram of an AI-native organisation: three stream-aligned pods, each with a PM and three engineers extended by agents and aligned to a customer domain, all supported by shared enabling and platform capabilities such as architecture, quality, design and UX, AI evaluation, CI/CD and observability](./ai-native-organisation.png)\\n\\n**The focus is always on the stream aligned teams**, all of the other team types are in service to them in order to deliver great software to the client. Importantly, we\'re not wanting every specialist function to review, approve or contribute to every feature. We want those specialist capabilities to enable the stream-aligned teams to operate with as much autonomy as possible.\\n\\n### It\'s about cognitive load, too\\n\\nOne of the key ideas in Team Topologies is *cognitive load*.\\n\\n:::note\\nEssentially, how much complexity does a team need to understand and manage to do its job?\\n:::\\n\\nThere\'s only so much information a group of people can reasonably keep in their heads. If a team needs to understand twenty different systems, five different technologies, a load of infrastructure and several completely unrelated business domains, it\'s probably going to struggle.\\n\\nIt\'ll spend enormous amounts of time trying to understand how everything fits together, rather than solving problems for customers.\\n\\nThe aim is to draw sensible boundaries around what a team owns, then provide the expertise and shared capabilities that allow it to be epic within those boundaries.\\n\\nGiven those team types, Team Topologies then describes three main interaction modes:\\n\\n- There\'s **collaboration**, where teams work closely together for a period to solve something, often because they\'re exploring something new or complicated.\\n- There\'s **facilitation**, where one team helps another develop a capability. For example, a quality engineering specialist working with a pod to help it adopt effective automated testing practices.\\n- And there\'s **X-as-a-Service**, where one team provides a capability that another can consume independently. Think of a deployment platform that allows a team to safely deploy its software without needing to involve the platform engineers every time.\\n\\nThese modes help us because **interaction has a cost**.\\n\\nCollaboration can be enormously valuable, but having five teams permanently collaborating on every feature is an expensive way to achieve very little.\\n\\nWe should be deliberate about where collaboration is needed, where expertise should be transferred, and where a shared self-service capability would make more sense. And importantly, **Team Topologies isn\'t just about drawing a lovely new organisational chart**. You can rename all your teams tomorrow, but if delivering a feature still requires seven handoffs, several approval meetings and a queue of work in three other departments, you\'ve not really achieved much. **It\'s the flow of work that matters.**\\n\\n***So why do I think all of this has become even more important with AI?***\\n\\n## Small teams, big problems\\n\\nThis isn\'t a hot take, but it\'s pretty clear that smaller, highly capable teams are the way to go for AI-native software delivery. Not quite to the extremes of the gurus on LinkedIn and the platform-formerly-known as Twitter, I\'m not suggesting we fire half our workforce and all have single engineer product teams. My preference is a pod consisting of **a great product manager and three-ish great, product-minded engineers**.\\n\\nNow, Team Topologies generally describes stable teams of around five to nine people. So yes, I\'m intentionally asking whether we still need teams of that size when every engineer has access to increasingly capable AI agents.\\n\\n***Why three engineers?***\\n\\nFirstly, I think there\'s a minimum number of humans you want involved to ensure there\'s diversity of thought, healthy technical challenge, knowledge sharing, and enough resilience when somebody goes on holiday. But there\'s also a point where adding more engineers starts creating more coordination overhead than the additional capacity is worth. And I think AI is changing where that point sits.\\n\\nImagine a team of ten engineers, each orchestrating five or ten coding agents. You\'ve potentially got an enormous amount of work being carried out simultaneously.\\n\\nSounds great!\\n\\nUntil you\'ve got agents making overlapping changes to codebases, engineers trying to understand what everybody else is working on, conflicting technical decisions, and a lot of effort going into keeping everybody aligned.\\n\\n**It gets messy, quickly.**\\n\\nSomething we\'ve noticed with larger teams is that they naturally start dividing themselves into smaller streams of work anyway. People group together around particular features or problems because it becomes too difficult for everybody to remain involved in everything.\\n\\nOf course, simply splitting a large team into smaller groups won\'t solve everything.\\n\\nIf all those teams are still constantly changing the same tightly coupled components, we\'ve just moved the problem somewhere else. This is where the *boundaries* are still as important as they ever were.\\n\\n### Own a customer problem, not a technical component\\n\\nYou don\'t want a frontend team, a backend team and a database team that all have to coordinate to deliver a feature. This gives you an organisational structure that requires handoffs for almost everything. I\'m not suggesting there\'s no place for deep technical specialism, or that every engineering team should look the same. That\'s partly why Team Topologies includes complicated-subsystem teams in the first place.\\n\\nBut for teams responsible for delivering customer value, we want them **aligned to customer problems and product domains**.\\n\\nThey should have a really clearly defined space that they\'re responsible for. They should understand their customers, understand the problems they\'re facing, and be empowered to decide how best to solve them. Ideally, they\'re able to do all of this with very little dependency on other teams. The underlying software architecture needs to support those same boundaries. If every change needs five teams to coordinate because of how we\'ve built the software, we\'re going to struggle regardless of how we\'ve drawn our team boundaries.\\n\\nBut here\'s where AI is making a positive change.\\n\\nPreviously, a team owning a reasonably substantial problem space might have needed quite a few engineers with different specialist skills to deliver within it. Now, those three engineers have a huge amount of additional capability available to them through AI. They can explore unfamiliar technologies, investigate parts of the codebase they don\'t know particularly well, build prototypes, implement solutions and validate their work at a pace that simply wasn\'t possible before.\\n\\nThat doesn\'t mean they suddenly become experts in everything. Far from it. But AI can fill in enough of those gaps that a smaller team can take on a much broader range of work. Our M-shaped engineers become comb-shaped.\\n\\n:::warning\\nNow I\'d like to make it super clear we\'re not making teams smaller for the sake of reducing headcount. We\'re making them small enough to work effectively, while giving them the capabilities they need to own meaningful customer problems. It we succeed at this, we can work on more of these problems at once. **\\"More with the same\\", rather than \\"the same with less\\", please.**\\n:::\\n\\nAnd it isn\'t a universal rule that every team must have exactly three engineers. Different problem spaces have different demands, and operational responsibilities matter too. A team running a critical service with significant out-of-hours responsibilities, for example, needs appropriate support arrangements. We shouldn\'t pretend that AI has magically removed the need for operational resilience. The important thing is that we\'re deliberately optimising the size and shape of our teams for the problem they\'re solving, with the new opportunity that those teams can be smaller, superpowered by AI covering their gaps.\\n\\n### AI changes the cognitive load equation\\n\\nThere\'s another interesting aspect to all of this.\\n\\n**AI can significantly reduce the cognitive load on engineers.**\\n\\nI no longer need to personally understand every detail of an unfamiliar technology before I can make progress. I can get an agent to help explore it, explain the relevant parts, investigate the codebase and implement a solid change.\\n\\nThat\'s an enormous advantage.\\n\\n**But AI also introduces new cognitive load.**\\n\\nIf I\'m running ten agents at once, I still need to understand what they\'re doing, check their important decisions, review their outputs and make sure the overall solution fits together. That\'s assuming I\'m not fully multi-agent vibe-coding (\\"make no mistakes\\").\\n\\nSo the goal isn\'t simply to give each engineer as many agents as possible and hope for the best. We need good boundaries around the work, sensible ways to coordinate parallel activity, and enough shared context that our agents aren\'t constantly working against one another. This is another reason why the enabling and platform team types become so important.\\n\\nWe want our small pods to have access to a huge range of capabilities, without expecting the engineers to become specialists in absolutely everything.\\n\\nBut before getting into those supporting functions, there\'s another aspect of the team that we haven\'t yet mentioned.\\n\\n## Engineers need to be product people too\\n\\nI\'ve not stopped going on about this for years, and I don\'t plan to! **Engineers should not be sitting around waiting for a product manager to tell them what to build.**\\n\\nIt\'s a huge red flag.\\n\\nI\'ve seen delivery processes where months and months of commercial conversations, product discovery and solution discussions happened before engineering were involved.\\n\\nBy the time the engineers saw the work, the decisions have largely been made. They\'ve got some requirements, perhaps some designs, and they\'re expected to turn those into software.\\n\\n![Diagram titled \\"Engineers join too late\\": a customer problem goes through months of disconnected discovery across customer-facing, business and product teams, then handoffs and queues, before engineering joins to build, test and release, resulting in long lead time, context loss and too many handoffs](./engineers-join-too-late.png)\\n\\n***Why?!***\\n\\nEngineers bring such a valuable perspective to those early conversations. They understand what\'s technically possible, what already exists within the system, where complexity might be hiding, and often have ideas for solving problems that are considerably simpler than the original proposal.\\n\\nAnd with AI tooling allowing us to explore those ideas so quickly, that perspective is becoming even more valuable.\\n\\n**The product manager and engineering lead should be a partnership.**\\n\\nBoth should be involved in customer conversations. Both should understand the problems being solved. Both should be contributing to what the solution looks like.\\n\\n![Diagram titled \\"Engineers join from the start\\": a cross-functional team including customer-facing, commercial, product and engineering people explores the customer problem together from day one, then moves straight into build, automated checks, release and customer feedback, giving shared context, fewer handoffs and faster feedback](./engineers-join-from-start.png)\\n\\nOf course, they\'ll have different strengths.\\n\\n- The PM will have a much deeper understanding of commercial considerations, product strategy and customer needs.\\n- The engineer will bring technical expertise, architectural thinking and an understanding of what\'s possible.\\n\\nThey should be challenging one another, a healthy friction.\\n\\nThe engineers in the pod should be just as interested in the customer problem as they are in the code they\'re writing. Otherwise, we\'ve built a small team that\'s still operating with a product-to-engineering handoff. We\'ve just made the handoff a little shorter.\\n\\n### AI should bring discovery and delivery closer together\\n\\nIt\'s amazing to see what AI prototyping tools are doing for product managers.\\n\\nThey can now explore ideas visually and communicate potential solutions without needing an engineer to build something for them.\\n\\nThat\'s hugely valuable.\\n\\n:::caution\\nThere\'s a potential trap here. Previously, a PM might hand engineering a Jira ticket and a Figma design. They could potentially hand engineering an AI-generated prototype and say, \\"Build this.\\" Have we actually improved the process? Or have we just made it easier to create a more convincing set of requirements?\\n:::\\n\\nThe way we should think about this is that those initial prototypes should be used to **explore ideas and communicate thinking**. They aren\'t necessarily the solution we\'re going to build.\\n\\nThis obviously has parallels with approaches like the [AI-Driven Development Lifecycle (AI-DLC)](https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/devops/ai-driven-development-life-cycle/), where product and engineering collaborate closely on understanding problems, elaborating solutions and capturing the context that agents need during construction.\\n\\n:::note\\nThere\'s a lot more to this than I\'ve got space for here. We\'ve been experimenting with our own approach to AI-DLC, including how to flow through the elaboration and construction phases with good feedback loops, and I want to dedicate a separate post to that.\\n:::\\n\\nFor now, the important thing is that **engineers are involved in discovering and shaping the solution**, rather than receiving something that\'s already been decided. And the whole pod retains ownership of the problem throughout delivery.\\n\\n## Your agents hate your handoffs\\n\\nOkay, your agents don\'t *hate* anything. But you get the idea.\\n\\nThis is one of the biggest reasons I think Team Topologies has become so important. We\'ve always known that handoffs slow software delivery, but with agentic engineering, handoffs don\'t just introduce waiting time. **They prevent agents from completing an otherwise automated workflow.**\\n\\nLet\'s use QA as an example.\\n\\nImagine you\'ve got Claude Code working on a feature. It implements the functionality, writes some automated tests, runs them, finds an issue, fixes it, and runs the tests again.\\n\\nIt can keep iterating through that loop, using the feedback to improve the implementation. This is one of the really powerful parts of agentic engineering. We\'re no longer just using AI to generate some code and then handing everything back to a human. We\'re allowing the agent to work through a problem, validate its own changes, discover issues and correct them.\\n\\nOf course, just because an agent has written a load of tests and made them all pass doesn\'t mean we\'ve built the right thing! We still need great quality engineering practices, meaningful acceptance criteria and ways of independently verifying that the software actually behaves as expected. But those checks should increasingly be available for engineers and agents to execute as part of their normal development workflow.\\n\\nNow imagine that regression testing for this feature requires somebody from a separate QA team to manually execute a test suite.\\n\\n***What happens to our lovely agentic feedback loop?***\\n\\n**It stops.**\\n\\nThe agent can\'t complete that loop independently. We\'re now waiting on somebody else to perform an activity before we can determine whether the change is good enough.\\n\\n![Diagram titled \\"Handoffs break the agent feedback loop\\": a traditional handoff where the agent builds, then waits for manual QA and sits in a queue, compared with an agent-compatible loop of build, test, verify and improve that runs continuously with human oversight](./agent-feedback-loops.png)\\n\\nThis is a handoff that we were trying to eliminate years ago anyway.\\n\\nBut now it\'s not just slowing down the engineer. It\'s preventing the agent from completing a much more substantial piece of work autonomously.\\n\\n**The handoffs we were already optimising away with Team Topologies are now actively limiting the depth and breadth of what our agents can accomplish.**\\n\\nAnd it\'s not just QA.\\n\\n- What happens when an agent needs architectural guidance, but the only way to get it is to book some time with an architect?\\n- What if our design standards aren\'t represented in code, and every change needs a designer to manually check whether we\'ve followed them?\\n- What if deploying software requires somebody to follow a set of manual steps in a portal?\\n\\nWe\'re constantly breaking the agent\'s ability to make progress.\\n\\nNow, I\'m not arguing that we should remove every human interaction from the process. Please don\'t give your coding agents unrestricted production access and tell them to crack on. At least not with anything that\'s holding my bank details. But there\'s an important distinction between **making a capability accessible to agents** and **giving those agents unrestricted authority**. For example, an agent might run security checks and verify compliance against our policies. That doesn\'t mean it has the authority to approve an exception to those policies.\\n\\nLikewise, an agent might prepare a change, but it still must go through an automated CI/CD pipeline, with validation ensuring that only successful changes are deployed to production. The pipeline provides the appropriate checks and controls, we didn\'t just give an agent owner premissions on our subscription and hope for the best.\\n\\nThe important thing is **designing the controls around the decisions that genuinely require human judgment**, rather than forcing people into every step of the process.\\n\\nIf something can be automated, made self-service, or verified without a manual handoff, why aren\'t we doing that? This was important 10 years ago. Now our new way of working is desperate for it.\\n\\nAnd that means rethinking how we provide the capabilities our engineering teams depend on.\\n\\n## Enabling teams have a new customer\\n\\nThis is where our previous thinking needs to evolve. We\'ve talked about small, stream-aligned pods, with clear ownership of customer problems. But we can\'t expect three engineers to be world-class experts in absolutely everything required to build great software. Architecture, quality engineering, security, scalability, observability, maintainability, design, user experience. And now increasingly, the quality and evaluation of AI features within our products.\\n\\nThere\'s an awful lot of expertise required, even for the AI-assisted legends we employ.\\n\\nThis is where our enabling teams come in, helping pods develop the capabilities they need without taking on unnecessary complexity. But I think there\'s a fundamental change in how these teams need to operate.\\n\\n:::info\\n**We can no longer think about enabling just the human engineer. We need to enable the engineer and their agents together.**\\n:::\\n\\nIf an enabling function provides something that is only usable by a human, we\'re actively blocking our teams from working with agents in the way they want to.\\n\\nTake architecture.\\n\\nWe\'ve always wanted architectural standards to be properly documented. Engineers should be able to find them, understand the decisions that have been made, and apply them when building software. Now we need those standards to be available to our agents too. That might mean making them easily discoverable within the repository, through agent instructions, or through tooling that provides relevant context.\\n\\nBut we can take it further.\\n\\nIt\'s one thing for an agent to read a document explaining how we expect our APIs to be secured. It\'s another thing entirely for that agent to have access to standard implementations and tooling that help it apply those practices, and automated checks that verify the result.\\n\\nThere are three things we want to enable.\\n\\n- **Knowledge:** Can the agent discover the standards and understand what good looks like?\\n- **Capabilities:** Can it access the tooling, components and workflows needed to build to those standards?\\n- **Verification:** Can it check that it\'s done things correctly, and use the feedback to fix anything that\'s wrong?\\n\\nThe third one is where your agent starts flying. Documentation is great, but agents can misunderstand things just like humans can. **Giving them a way to verify their work is a much more reliable way of ensuring quality.**\\n\\n### This applies to all of our specialist functions\\n\\nTake design and UX.\\n\\nA great design system should have well-defined components that engineers can use to build consistent experiences. We\'ve wanted that for years, and many companies have been building them out. Now we should be making those components available to our agents too, so they can build interfaces following our design conventions without needing a designer to guide every step. And could we provide verifiers that check accessibility standards have been fully adhered to? Of course, this doesn\'t remove the need for great designers. We still need people who understand user journeys, interaction design and the more subjective parts of creating a great experience.\\n\\nBut do we really want them spending their days correcting the same component implementation mistakes over and over again?\\n\\nOr quality engineering.\\n\\nWe don\'t want QA specialists sitting at the end of the delivery process testing every feature. We want them helping our pods build great quality practices into everything they do. Automated testing frameworks, load testing strategies, reliable test environments, and tooling that both engineers and agents can use to validate their work. And we need ways of independently verifying that those tests are actually checking the right things, not simply trusting that an agent has produced a load of green ticks.\\n\\nFor AI products, we have another dimension of quality to consider. We need good evaluation practices, appropriate metrics and the ability to continuously understand whether the AI features we\'re delivering are actually performing well.\\n\\nThe same applies to security, infrastructure, observability and all the other specialist capabilities we need.\\n\\nThis isn\'t a significant diversion, but it requires a nice evolution in how enabling functions view their responsibilities.\\n\\n**Their job isn\'t just to share their expertise with human engineers. It\'s to make that expertise available in a way that supports the entire human-agent team.**\\n\\nAnd for some capabilities, that\'s going to involve building shared tooling and platforms. So we should probably talk about those...\\n\\n## Please don\'t build a platform for everything\\n\\nI\'ve [written about paved paths before](https://christaceygreen.com/blog/paved-paths-series-part-2-a-one-pager). I\'m a big believer in making the well-trodden route the easiest, safest and quickest way to reach a destination. And platform engineering is clearly an incredibly important part of making stream-aligned teams successful. A good internal platform can remove an enormous amount of complexity from our pods. Think about everything involved in deploying and running a modern application. Infrastructure, identity and permissions, networking, deployment pipelines, monitoring, secrets management, and many other things we\'ve all smashed our brains against at 2am.\\n\\nDo we really want every pod building and maintaining all of that independently?\\n\\nOf course not.\\n\\nA platform can provide those shared capabilities in a consistent, self-service way, leaving our engineers and agents to focus on the problems that actually differentiate our product.\\n\\nBut I\'ve also seen how easy it is to get carried away building platforms. Especially now that AI makes building software much easier. You\'ve identified a common problem across a couple of teams? Fantastic, let\'s build a centralised platform to solve it!\\n\\n**Hold up.**\\n\\nA platform isn\'t just something you build once and get to forget about. You\'ve got to maintain it, support it, improve it, keep it secure, and make sure it continues to meet the needs of the teams consuming it. There\'s a cost attached to all of that. There\'s also something else that you risk by building platforms too early...\\n\\n:::caution\\n**You might be standardising something you don\'t yet understand.**\\n:::\\n\\nRemember the woodland from my paved paths posts?\\n\\nWe agreed that we don\'t want everybody cutting their own route through the forest every time they\'re trying to get to the same destination. That\'s wasteful, risky, and results in somebody getting eaten by a bear. But equally, we don\'t want to pave a road to the wrong destination simply because somebody thought it looked like the right direction.\\n\\nSometimes we need people exploring the woodland before we know which routes are worth paving. I think this applies really nicely to enabling teams. The preference should be to **start by enabling our pods to develop a capability**. Have the specialists work with them, share expertise, establish good practices and make those practices accessible to their agents.\\n\\nAs we do this, we\'ll learn where the common problems are.\\n\\n- Perhaps we\'re repeatedly helping different pods solve the same issue.\\n- Perhaps they\'re all building very similar tooling.\\n- Or perhaps there\'s a chunk of complexity that really shouldn\'t be something every team needs to understand.\\n\\n**Now we have a reason to consider a platform.**\\n\\n### AI evaluation is a good example\\n\\nWe all know by now that when building AI products, we need to evaluate how well those AI features actually perform. When you first start building AI features into products, it\'s really difficult to know what a common evaluation platform should look like. Different product domains have different requirements. What makes a good result for one AI feature might be completely different from another. The tooling is evolving quickly, the evaluation techniques are evolving, and you\'re still learning what good looks like for your particular products.\\n\\n***So should we immediately build a central platform and force everybody to use it?***\\n\\n**No.**\\n\\nAllow teams to explore these approaches within their own problem spaces first. Initially, there simply isn\'t enough shared understanding to know what the right common abstraction would look like. But after enough experience across different AI product domains, patterns will start to emerge. The evaluation criteria might still be specific to each product, but much of the underlying approach, tooling and practice is common. Suddenly, a shared evaluation platform starts making a lot of sense. The platform can provide the evaluation infrastructure, common tooling and established practices, and the individual pods can configure what quality means for their particular domain, while retaining responsibility for the outcome.\\n\\n**You standardise the bits that are common, without taking away the team\'s ability to make the decisions that matter for its product.**\\n\\n![Diagram titled \\"From enabling expertise to a platform\\": enable teams by sharing expertise, learn by observing repeated needs, then decide whether a platform is worth the cost; if yes, build a properly owned self-service platform for engineers and agents, if not, keep enabling. AI evaluation is shown as an example](./enable-then-platform.png)\\n\\n### When should enablement become a platform capability?\\n\\nI\'m not suggesting that every enabling team should eventually become a platform team. They have different responsibilities, and Team Topologies is quite deliberate about that distinction. But I do think every enabling function should regularly question whether the way it\'s providing its expertise is still the most effective approach. Sometimes the answer will be to continue helping pods develop their own capabilities. Sometimes it\'ll become clear that we\'re repeatedly solving the same problems, and a shared capability could reduce an enormous amount of duplicated effort. The specialists in an enabling function are often ideally placed to identify those opportunities, because they\'ve already spent time understanding where teams are struggling.\\n\\nSo I\'d like to see these functions regularly asking themselves some questions.\\n\\n- Are multiple pods solving the same problem?\\n- Have we learnt enough to define a shared approach without constraining teams unnecessarily?\\n- Would a platform genuinely reduce cognitive load and dependencies?\\n- And would the benefit justify the ongoing cost of maintaining it?\\n\\nIf the answers point towards a platform, great. Let\'s deliberately make that investment, with proper ownership and responsibility for maintaining it. That might involve the enabling specialists helping to establish a dedicated platform team. Or the organisation might already have a platform function that\'s best placed to take ownership of the new capability.\\n\\nThe important thing is that **we\'re making the decision consciously**.\\n\\nWe really don\'t want to end up with an accidental platform, where a team has built so many helpful scripts and services that the entire organisation now depends on them, but nobody\'s actually taken responsibility for maintaining them as a product. And importantly, when we do build these platforms, **they need to support both humans and agents**. A shiny internal developer portal might be lovely for our engineers, but it\'s not much use to a coding agent if all the important capabilities require somebody to click through a UI. The underlying capabilities should be available through appropriate APIs, tools and automated workflows, with sensible access controls and guardrails.\\n\\nWe\'re trying to make the paved path easy to follow for the agent as well as the engineer. Our robot friend needs to walk through that forest safely too.\\n\\n## We can\'t ignore DevOps and CI/CD\\n\\nThere\'s something important I haven\'t really explored yet. We\'ve talked a lot about how to structure teams, but the way those teams deliver and operate software is just as important. After all, it\'s no good having an autonomous stream-aligned pod if every change still needs to pass through a separate operations team before it can reach production.\\n\\nIn the age of AI, this becomes even more important, and we have new questions to answer.\\n\\n- How do we handle the increased volume of changes produced by agents?\\n- How should CI pipelines evolve to support multiple agents working in parallel?\\n- What does a great automated testing strategy look like when agents can write both the implementation and the tests?\\n- How do we safely give agents access to deployment and operational capabilities?\\n\\nThere\'s a lot to unpack there, and rather than doing a terrible job of squeezing all of that into this post, I want to explore DevOps, CI/CD and software quality in the age of AI properly in later parts of this series. For now, the important point is that **our organisational structure needs to support end-to-end ownership, and our engineering practices need to make that ownership possible**.\\n\\nOne without the other isn\'t going to get us very far.\\n\\n## How do we know whether it\'s working?\\n\\nSo we\'ve got small, empowered teams. They\'re close to their customers. They have clear ownership of a problem space, and they\'re supported by enabling and platform functions that make it easier for both engineers and agents to deliver great software. How do we know whether any of this is actually improving things?\\n\\nI\'m still a big fan of [DORA metrics](https://dora.dev/guides/dora-metrics/) for understanding software delivery performance.\\n\\nFor anyone unfamiliar, they give us a way to understand how effectively we\'re delivering software. Things like how frequently we deploy, how long code changes take to reach production, how often those changes cause problems, how much rework we\'re creating, and how quickly we recover when deployments fail.\\n\\nI\'d also look at the [SPACE framework](https://doi.org/10.1145/3454122.3454124), which encourages us to think about engineering productivity more broadly, including things like developer satisfaction, collaboration, performance and flow.\\n\\nAnd, of course, the commercial and product metrics associated with the products our teams are building.\\n\\nI\'m going to do a separate post about measuring software delivery, but I want to quickly talk about one metric today.\\n\\n**The time from identifying a customer problem through to delivering and validating the solution.**\\n\\nNot the time from a code commit to production.\\n\\nThe whole journey.\\n\\nDORA\'s change lead time measures the journey from code being committed through to deployment in production. That\'s valuable, but I\'m interested in the much bigger picture. I\'ve actually measured this longer lead time before, and I have scars because it hasn\'t been particularly useful for engineers.\\n\\n***Why?***\\n\\nBecause they had so little influence over much of it!\\n\\nIf months of product discovery have happened before engineering gets involved, how can we reasonably expect engineers to take care about the metric?\\n\\n**They can\'t change what they aren\'t involved in.**\\n\\nBut now we\'re talking about pods that are much closer to customers, with engineers participating in discovery, challenging solutions, and owning delivery through to validation. Suddenly, that longer lead time becomes much more meaningful. We want to understand the full time from a customer problem being identified to having a validated solution, and use that metric to understand whether our overall delivery flow is improving. As with all of the previously mentioned metrics, we should always **consider the context behind the number**. A problem that requires substantial customer discovery, difficult technical decisions or significant experimentation is naturally going to take longer than a straightforward improvement.\\n\\nThat doesn\'t automatically make the team less effective.\\n\\nSometimes spending longer understanding the problem is exactly what leads to a better, simpler solution. What we want is for the pod to understand why something took as long as it did, and whether there\'s anything it could improve next time.\\n\\n- Perhaps there were unnecessary handoffs.\\n- Perhaps decisions were repeatedly revisited because we didn\'t have the right people involved early enough.\\n- Perhaps our agents were getting stuck waiting for manual processes.\\n\\nOr perhaps the problem was genuinely difficult, and the team spent its time doing exactly the right things. As with all of the quantitative metrics, the context behind them should be part of the same conversation. And this is why I\'d look at that longer lead time alongside delivery quality, developer experience and the commercial outcomes we\'re aiming to achieve. This sets us up to avoid measuring whether we produce more software and instead measure whether we **solve customer problems more effectively.**\\n\\n## This is just the beginning (how ominous)\\n\\nI want to finish by coming back to where I started. We can see that agentic engineering is producing engineers that are able to deliver an extraordinary amount of value. But we mustn\'t focus solely on this part of the problem. We\'re talking about how many agents an engineer can run, how quickly they can implement features, and which model is currently winning the coding benchmarks.\\n\\nAll useful things to explore.\\n\\nBut if those engineers are still operating within a delivery process full of handoffs, disconnected discovery, poor feedback loops and slow decision-making, how much of that additional capability are we actually benefiting from? We\'ve potentially made the engineering part of the process faster, while leaving the rest of it largely unchanged. And I think that\'s where a lot of our brain power should be focusing now. Thankfully, most of these topics aren\'t new, they\'re fundamental engineering practices that just need a little refresh.\\n\\n- **Team Topologies** has been encouraging us to create small, empowered teams with clear ownership and minimal dependencies for years.\\n- **DevOps** has been encouraging us to take shared responsibility for building, deploying and operating our software.\\n- **CI/CD** has been encouraging us to make small changes, automate our quality checks, integrate frequently and keep our software in a state where it can be released safely.\\n- And good **product engineering** has been encouraging us to get engineers closer to customers and make decisions based on a genuine understanding of their problems.\\n\\nThese all become **more important, not less**, with AI agents working alongside our engineers.\\n\\nThere are still plenty of questions I\'m working through.\\n\\n- How small should our teams really be?\\n- Where\'s the right balance between mobbing around problems, and making small iterative changes?\\n- How do we manage the cognitive load of multiple agents?\\n- When is it worth building a platform instead of continuing to enable teams directly?\\n\\nI don\'t think we\'ve figured all of that out yet, and I expect the answers will keep changing as the problem evolves.\\n\\nBut I\'m increasingly confident that the organisations getting the most value from AI aren\'t the ones with the fastest engineers. **They\'re the ones that have optimised their entire software delivery process to take advantage of that speed.**\\n\\nIn the next posts, I want to dig into the engineering practices that make this possible. DevOps, CI/CD, automated quality, AI evaluation and our evolving approach to AI-DLC.\\n\\nIf you\'ve made it this far, thanks for reading! I\'m genuinely interested in how others are approaching this, particularly those experimenting with smaller teams and agentic delivery. I\'m sure there are plenty of things we haven\'t figured out yet.\\n\\nUntil next time!"},{"id":"attention-to-detail-is-all-you-need","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/attention-to-detail-is-all-you-need","editUrl":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen/ctaceygreen.github.io/blog/2025-06-11-attention-to-detail-is-all-you-need/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-11-attention-to-detail-is-all-you-need/index.md","title":"Attention (to detail) is all you need","description":"How to differentiate yourself and your business in an AI-generated world","date":"2025-06-11T00:00:00.000Z","formattedDate":"June 11, 2025","tags":[{"label":"AI","permalink":"/blog/tags/ai"},{"label":"GenAI","permalink":"/blog/tags/gen-ai"},{"label":"usp","permalink":"/blog/tags/usp"},{"label":"detail","permalink":"/blog/tags/detail"},{"label":"leadership","permalink":"/blog/tags/leadership"}],"readingTime":12.095,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[{"name":"Chris Tacey-Green","title":"Engineer, Architect, Human","url":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen","image_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"attention-to-detail-is-all-you-need","title":"Attention (to detail) is all you need","description":"How to differentiate yourself and your business in an AI-generated world","authors":[{"name":"Chris Tacey-Green","title":"Engineer, Architect, Human","url":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen","image_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4"}],"image":"https://christaceygreen.com/2025-06-11-attention-to-detail-is-all-you-need/title-image.png","tags":["AI","GenAI","usp","detail","leadership"]},"prevItem":{"title":"Your Engineers Are AI-Native. Is Your Organisation?","permalink":"/blog/your-engineers-are-ai-native-is-your-organisation"},"nextItem":{"title":"Paved Paths Series - Part 4 - The Anatomy of Paved Paths","permalink":"/blog/paved-paths-series-part-4-the-anatomy-of-paved-paths"}},"content":"![title image reading \\"The anatomy of paved paths\\" with lego behind it](/2025-06-11-attention-to-detail-is-all-you-need/title-image.png)\\n\\n:::info Oh the irony\\nThis was the first image that came up when I asked ChatGPT to generate an image for this blog post. I thought it was too beautifully ironic to ignore. Maybe AI really is funny?\\n:::\\n\\n## Context\\n\\nFirstly, some important context. I use GenAI tooling almost every hour of my waking day. I think it has incredible value already, and huge amounts of future potential as we build around it. I\'m not one of the people saying the tech is a stochastic parrot and has no value. I\'m very much on the hype train. \\n\\nAlso, not that credentials are required in order to have an opinion, but I have some old-school experience with AI. I was working on ML algorithms over 10 years ago. I remember the excitement I had when I discovered GANs (Generative Adversarial Networks) and was convinced this was our path to superintelligence. The first GPT I started typing away with was GPT3 (we\'ve come a long way).\xa0 More recently, I\'ve been building multi-agent platforms in my spare time. I\'m not going to write about any of that today, though. There are many many many people who\'ve written great pieces on how the tech works. I may write on the tech in another post, but not today! This braindump is much \'softer\', based on where I see the technology industry going, and crucially, where I think individuals and organisations can differentiate themselves in this environment.\\n\\n:::info A note on AI usage within this post\\nI started writing this blog with AI. I then questioned what the point was of doing that. I write in order to bring my ideas together. It\'s not necessarily about the produced blog post; there\'s value in the writing process. So I used AI to help with a suggested structure, and to help bring some of my voice-notes-whilst-driving together. I then deleted the whole lot and re-wrote it once I had the structure in my head. The only input AI has had in the finished post is correcting any terrible grammar, but I ensured it left the wording raw. It will read like it\'s come out of my mouth, rather than a beautiful piece of AI generated content. I hope that feels refreshing, and real :)\\n:::\\n\\n## The Vibes\\n\\nLovable. Replit. v0. Bolt. If you\'ve been anywhere near the AI conversation online, you\'ll have seen wild claims of someone using these tools to \\"Build a $10m ARR SaaS Product in 2 days\\". If we ignore the exaggeration, and the heaps of clickbait, there is genuine value in the tooling. A lot of humans benefit from visual aids when making decisions. There\'s a reason that presentation decks exists; they help to tell stories and deliver thoughts where a written memo would be lacking. ([apart from Amazon apparently, who only use memos (this has been disputed by internal employees)](https://www.cnbc.com/2018/04/23/what-jeff-bezos-learned-from-requiring-6-page-memos-at-amazon.html)). By getting all stakeholders in a room, smashing thoughts into Lovable and seeing the results of your stream of your discussions, you **will**\xa0drive better and faster decision-making. It\'s the agile dream, what a feedback loop!\\n\\nHowever, we must remind stakeholders that prototypes are not working software.\xa0\\n\\nOne of these vibe-coding tools (I will keep them nameless) emails out a newsletter showcasing AI-built apps on their platform. 5 \'top picks\' from the thousands that had been generated. As I tapped through on my phone, the lack of care smacked me in the face. Two of the apps loaded with errors. Another two weren\'t responsive for mobile, so loaded with overflowing text and buttons all over the place.  Only one app genuinely delivered. Now, is the tooling going to improve? 100%. But I was shocked that these were the top picks, chosen to advertise the power of vibe-coding. Remember, this post isn\'t about the tooling. It will change. What we need to talk about is what differentiates you in this new world...\\n\\nAttention to Detail.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Attention to Detail\\n\\nThere are three areas I\'m going to pick on as examples, although I think we could create a much longer list.\\n\\n### **Attention to Detail on Facts**\\n\\nGenAI is hugely disrupting search. Perplexity popped up as an answer engine, and demonstrated what the combination of LLMs and traditional search could do. No need to click through and read 10 different web pages from your Google search, Perplexity will do that for you and synthesise it all into a beautiful answer. More recently, the big LLM providers are all adding research (or deep research) functionalities, which can spend minutes researching a topic for you, bringing together information and sources, and presenting it to you.\xa0\\n\\n***So why do we need to pay attention here?***\\n\\nEven with the great work these agentic approaches pump out, hallucinations are still common. I have no doubt that this will improve in the products, but I still regularly get research responses where citations and quotes from sources have been entirely fabricated. How do I know? Because I check them. And you should too. You\'ve saved an awful lot of time in aggregating this information, please spend an appropriate amount of time fact-checking it. Paying attention to the detail within the generated content will, unfortunately,  differentiate you from a large number of people, who blindly take these responses as facts. As an aside, I\'ve worked a lot on building out multi-agent frameworks that can reduce these poor citations. There are flows that can help, but for now, fact-check yourself. You will still be accountable for whatever you do with this information, so you should have a healthy amount of skepticism and apply critical thinking. This will give you the best of both worlds: significantly less monotonous work, and high-quality outputs that reflect your care and skill.\\n\\n### Attention to Detail on Working Software\\n\\nEveryone (with the financial means) now has access to vibe-coding tools, and AI Coding Agents (Cursor, Windsurf, Github Copilot, Manus etc). Time to POC has plummeted. So in a world where everyone can build an app, how do you differentiate yourself? It\'s the detail and care over the software you\'re building. I\'m not suggesting you need to think about these things when you\'re living the agile dream, prototype-vibing with your stakeholders. Focus on the decisions that need to be made then. But once those initial questions have been answered, you **are not done**. \\n- Have you genuinely thought about the features you\'re building, or were they simply features the AI decided would be good? \\n- Have you thought about the integrations and data you need to make this real? \\n- Have you thought about how this software is going to run across the multitude of platforms that your users will expect to use it on? \\n- Have you considered the security model that needs to be applied to each of the components of your workload? \\n- What scale are you wanting to build for at this point in time, and  do the decisions you\'ve made align with that? \\n- Have you thought about the user experience you\'re trying to achieve here, or have you simply accepted the generic template that the vibes built? (more on user experience later).\xa0\\n\\nThe list goes on.\\n\\nThis, in my opinion, is where you differentiate yourself from others. Use the power of these tools, and then take them to the next level. Use your experience. Use your knowledge of the problem space. I genuinely think that by doing this, you\'ll produce exceptional software, at a pace that you never could previously. And crucially, it will stand out amongst the sea of AI generated software where this care wasn\'t taken.\\n\\n#### Side note - Code accountability\\n\\nThis really shouldn\'t need saying. Whether your code came from Lovable, Cursor, Claude or StackOverflow, **you are accountable for it**. This does not change. It will feel a little odd at first. The code in your IDE has been written by someone else. But if you think about it, this is something we\'ve all been doing for years right? Your team members don\'t write code in the same way as you, but you review it, you pair with them on it, until you\'re at a point that you\'re happy to have your name stamped against it alongside them. That\'s exactly what you should be doing here, if you\'re applying attention to detail. \\n\\n:::caution \\nThose who are fully vibe deploying to production - you are wild. I hope you only do this for online games, and nothing that handles my payment processing...\\n:::\\n\\n### Attention to Detail on User Experience\\n\\nIf you\'ve been using vibe-coding tools for a while, you\'ll find that you can spot similar apps in the wild quite easily. \\n\\n*\\"Ah that\'s a Replit app\\"* \\n\\n*\\"That looks like it was from Lovable\\"* \\n\\nThey are intentionally opinionated, which is why they create such incredible applications in such a short amount of time. Those opinions also mean that they\'re less flexible and less creative when it comes to your problem. They have standard components to apply. **The niggling feeling in the back of my head kicks in here**. I think this generic nature of applications works if you\'re using these UIs for internal use-cases (e.g. Making it easier for an operational team to do a certain task). But when we\'re talking about our paying customers, in a lot of cases this is going to **prevent your organisation from differentiating**. \\n\\nIf you simply need a shopfront for your hand-built tables, your differentiation is your woodworking skill. But in a lot of SaaS applications, **your differentiation is\xa0your user experience**. You win because users love it when they visit your site. They feel comfortable. It feels intuitive. They \'feel\' your brand. This comes from that oh-so-fluffy world of UX. It comes from designing the journeys that your users take through the application. **It comes from attention to detail**. As with all of my caveats, the vibe-coding tools may well become easier to give this flair to your applications. But it should **never**\xa0remove the responsibility from you. As soon as that happens, you\'ve stopped thinking about your users. I think caring deeply about your user experience is going to be one of the key things that differentiates you and your organisation going forward. Hold onto this, prioritise this, and you\'ll see long-term value.\\n\\n#### A side note on user experience\\n\\nCan we talk about chatbots? I get the feeling that everyone thinks adding chatbots to their website is an amazing idea and we all want that. I\'m not sure about you, but **I can\'t think of anything worse**. If our idea of utopia is that now, instead of opening 5 websites and using a well-designed journey through it, I now open 5 websites and hope for the best that my worded message to the chatbot is understood correctly: **I\'m out**. Don\'t get me wrong, I\'m all-in on agents doing tasks for me. But it shouldn\'t be an agent per website that knows nothing about me. It should be a personal agent, that uses APIs (or MCP if you like) to integrate with the 5 services for me, through one single agent experience. Maybe I\'m out on my own with this opinion, but I really do not want website-specific chatbots, and I already avoid them wherever I can.\\n\\n#### A second side note on user experience\\n\\nI keep hearing customer service being touted as the *great opportunity for organisations to automate with AI*. Why?! For a lot of companies, **customer service is one of the rare opportunities that you get to actually communicate with your customers**. It\'s **so**\xa0hard to get feedback and interaction with customers. It\'s something that startups are desperate for. And you\'re going to automate this away with an AI Agent?! \\n\\nThis leads me onto my bonus differentiation:\\n\\n### **Being great at interacting with other humans**\\n\\n**For individuals**, this is something that will become more and more important. Certainly within the tech world. You may not need to be typing away as much code in your day, but your knowledge of the business, the processes, your users and their pain points, is all going to be a differentiator for you. **You will produce better AI-generated solutions because of it**.\\n\\n**For organisations**, I\'m convinced that **AI fatigue is going to hit all of your users within the next few years**. They\'re going to be tired of interacting with AI agents during their workday, only to talk to more of them when they\'re trying to get help from your company. I think once the excitement about chatbots has died down, the organisations that have prioritised human interaction in the client-facing parts of the offering, will **thrive**. They will be seen as unique. People will appreciate speaking to a human. They\'ll find it refreshing, and it\'ll feel like a premium experience.\\n\\n***Okay, so how do we make that a reality?***\\n\\nPeople are expensive, and replacing them with AI Agents will be entirely achievable in the near future. But let\'s just think this through for a minute longer. My suggestion would be to **automate everything behind the human**. Make it that they don\'t need to fight with systems, trawl through data, and spend half their day doing administrative work. **Build agents into their interactions**, so that the human is getting hints, answers and realtime data fed to them as they\'re talking to the user. **Make them superhuman.** Don\'t make them a robot. I\'m not saying this for empathetic reasons, I\'m saying it from a commercial perspective. I\'m betting that in a few years time, people will be desperate for human-interaction.\\n\\n### Final Thoughts: Attention to Detail is your Differentiator\\n\\nIn a world where more and more of the world is generated by AI, **attention to detail will become your differentiator**. As an individual, this means **fact-checking** and holding tight onto your **accountability of what you produce with AI**. It means **caring** and paying more attention to your user experience. As an organisation, it means all of this, plus being **crystal clear where your brand differentiates**. And if you are lucky enough to interact with your users, **do not replace that interaction with AI!**\\n\\nIf you read to the end, thanks for spending that time! I\'m genuinely interested in your thoughts, so ping me wherever you find most convenient. This was a bit of a braindump of the pinball thoughts that have been bouncing around my head for a few weeks. Less structured, less deliberate, but quite fun to write! \\n\\nUntil next time x"},{"id":"paved-paths-series-part-4-the-anatomy-of-paved-paths","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/paved-paths-series-part-4-the-anatomy-of-paved-paths","editUrl":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen/ctaceygreen.github.io/blog/2023-05-21-paved-paths-04-anatomy-of-paved-paths/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2023-05-21-paved-paths-04-anatomy-of-paved-paths/index.md","title":"Paved Paths Series - Part 4 - The Anatomy of Paved Paths","description":"Part 4 of the series - A deeper dive into the inner workings of paved paths","date":"2023-05-21T00:00:00.000Z","formattedDate":"May 21, 2023","tags":[{"label":"paved-path","permalink":"/blog/tags/paved-path"},{"label":"anatomy","permalink":"/blog/tags/anatomy"},{"label":"inner-workings","permalink":"/blog/tags/inner-workings"},{"label":"building-blocks","permalink":"/blog/tags/building-blocks"},{"label":"blueprints","permalink":"/blog/tags/blueprints"},{"label":"funnels","permalink":"/blog/tags/funnels"},{"label":"culture","permalink":"/blog/tags/culture"}],"readingTime":10.71,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Chris Tacey-Green","title":"Engineer, Architect, Human","url":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen","image_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4"},{"name":"Rick Roche","title":"Software Engineer, Solutions Architect, Hiking Enthusiast","url":"https://www.rickroche.com/","image_url":"https://www.rickroche.com/images/avatar.png","imageURL":"https://www.rickroche.com/images/avatar.png"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"paved-paths-series-part-4-the-anatomy-of-paved-paths","title":"Paved Paths Series - Part 4 - The Anatomy of Paved Paths","description":"Part 4 of the series - A deeper dive into the inner workings of paved paths","authors":[{"name":"Chris Tacey-Green","title":"Engineer, Architect, Human","url":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen","image_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4"},{"name":"Rick Roche","title":"Software Engineer, Solutions Architect, Hiking Enthusiast","url":"https://www.rickroche.com/","image_url":"https://www.rickroche.com/images/avatar.png","imageURL":"https://www.rickroche.com/images/avatar.png"}],"image":"https://christaceygreen.com/2023-05-21-paved-paths-04-anatomy-of-paved-paths/title-image.png","tags":["paved-path","anatomy","inner-workings","building-blocks","blueprints","funnels","culture"]},"prevItem":{"title":"Attention (to detail) is all you need","permalink":"/blog/attention-to-detail-is-all-you-need"},"nextItem":{"title":"Paved Paths Series - Part 2 - A One Pager","permalink":"/blog/paved-paths-series-part-2-a-one-pager"}},"content":"![title image reading \\"The anatomy of paved paths\\" with lego behind it](/2023-05-21-paved-paths-04-anatomy-of-paved-paths/title-image.png)\\n\\n:::info Paved Path Series\\nThis is Part 4 in a series of posts discussing Paved Paths between [Rick](https://www.rickroche.com/) and I.\\n- [Part 1 - Paved Paths: Let\'s talk about paved paths](https://www.rickroche.com/2023/04/paved-paths-series-part-1-lets-talk-about-paved-paths)\\n- [Part 2 - Paved Paths: One Pager](https://christaceygreen.com/blog/paved-paths-series-part-2-a-one-pager)\\n- [Part 3 - Paved Paths: Why Paved Paths?](https://www.rickroche.com/2023/05/paved-paths-series-part-3-why-paved-paths)\\n- [Part 5 - Paved Paths: The Spectrum of Platform Engineering and Paved Paths](https://www.rickroche.com/2023/06/paved-paths-series-part-5-the-spectrum/)\\n:::\\n\\nIn this post, we explore the inner workings of what makes up a paved path, aiming to demystify the components that will help you to realise the characteristics of a good paved path.\\n\\n# What are they made of?\\n\\nPaved paths will tend to be specific to your organisation, your tech stack and your principles but there are several questions that they should be able to answer, spanning four categories\\n- Building Blocks\\n- Blueprints & Funnels\\n- Documentation and Onboarding\\n- Culture\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n\\n![Venn diagram of the intersection of four circles, Docs & Onboarding, Blueprints & funnels, Building blocks, and Culture](paved-path-venn.png)\\n\\n\\n## Building blocks\\n\\n### Categorise your building blocks\\n\\n:::info Answers the questions:\\n- When I need to perform \\"X\\" in my app/service, what tool/technology do I use?\\n- I want to \\"do X\\", do we have something in the organisation that enables that already?\\n- Do we have multiple technologies/tools doing the same thing? How do I know what to choose? What is the tools\' lifecycle?\\n- To adopt this approach, what cultural changes are needed in the team?\\n:::\\n\\nThere are a general set of styles of building blocks that are useful here:\\n\\n**Infrastructure building blocks** - ingress (load balancing, WAF etc), compute, secrets / config management, feature flags etc\\n- Think of all the components you need to bring your architecture to life\\n\\n**Application building blocks** - internal libraries, common project structure, scaffolded apps with add-ins for observability, authN/Z, using config / secrets / feature flags etc\\n- e.g. Spring Initializr for Spring Boot projects or dotnet templates\\n\\n**Workflow / process building blocks** - Dev(SecFin...)Ops\\n- Things that run in our pipelines - start small and increment here\\n\\t- e.g. Dependency management, SAST, SCA, tests, linters, cost management etc\\n\\n:::caution\\nMake this a rule:\xa0**If it\'s not part of your pipeline, it doesn\'t exist**\\n:::\\n\\nWrite these building blocks down, they form a set of outlines that your paved paths aim to fill out over time, a roadmap of sorts. This can be as simple as a list on a wiki, modelled in software (Enterprise Architecture tools etc), loaded into an Internal Developer Portal or put into a picture. Make it accessible to all, easy to navigate and easy to update.\\n\\n![](paved-paths-anatomy-categories.png)\\n\\nIn your organisation, you will most likely find components that are fulfilled by multiple applications / libraries, a simple way to highlight the lifecycle of these is to use a technology radar and [Build your Own Radar](https://www.thoughtworks.com/radar/byor). This helps teams to decide what they should be phasing in, and phasing out.\\n\\n### How to structure your building blocks\\n\\nWhenever we think of building blocks, LEGO comes to mind. Keeping the building blocks composable is a great idea - you don\'t want your paved paths to become a new ball of mud.\\n\\nFor **infrastructure** building blocks, outputs could be layered Infrastructure as Code (IaC) modules\\n- Tier 1 - discrete components. e.g. a load balancer module, a WAF module\\n- Tier 2 - \u201cLEGO\u201d style building blocks, using tier 1. e.g. an application load balancer with WAF\\n-  Tier 3 - \u201cDUPLO\u201d style building blocks that enable infra needed to realise a blueprint - one click accelerators using tier 1 and 2. e.g. a public facing static web application\\n\\nFor **workflow / process** building blocks, outputs could be a pipeline template library\\n- Tier 1 - discrete jobs. e.g. build container, scan container, push to registry\\n- Tier 2 - \u201cLEGO\u201d style building blocks, orchestrating tier 1. e.g. build &rarr; scan &rarr; push to registry\\n-  Tier 3 - \u201cDUPLO\u201d style building blocks that enable common stages in a pipeline using tier 1 and tier 2\\n- Tier 4 - full end-to-end pipelines for specific blueprints or generic pipeline structures. e.g. Full CI/CD spanning multiple environments\\n\\nAdding extensibility points to all pipeline templates in tiers 3/4 will help to avoid duplication where teams want \\"almost\\" whats there.\\n\\nFor **application** building blocks, outputs could be internal code libraries, such as `npm` or `nuget` packages\\n- Tier 1 - base functionality, following engineering standards. e.g. Logging implementation. Authentication middleware.\\n- Tier 2 - \\"LEGO\\" style building blocks, using tier 1, combining into a common bootstrapping library. e.g. ServiceCollection extension methods for a REST API setting up auth middleware and standard logging of HTTP requests.\\n- Tier 3 - \\"DUPLO\\" style building blocks, using tier 1 and tier 2, that implement a common pattern or process. e.g. A base class for event-driven handlers, implementing an inbox pattern and standard event handling methods.\\n\\nThink about what guard rails can be put in place for each building block. We like the \\"prevent / detect / guide\\" model:\\n- **Prevent** controls stop teams from doing things that violate a policy or standard. E.g. preventing the creation of a load balancer exposing HTTP or preventing the push of a build of an image that has critical vulnerabilities to your registry.\\n- **Detect** controls allow teams to do things that may not be perfect, or mitigated by other controls but allow them to proceed and then a secondary process monitors them.\\n- **Guide** controls are simply guidance and lessons learnt in using different components.\\n\\nThe goal is to enable teams to move quickly and safely, enabling them to compose their own high-quality solutions using the building blocks. This is where we would push for the inclusion of standards, to make it easy for teams to do the \\"right things\\".\\n\\n\\n## Blueprints and funnels\\n\\n:::info Answers the questions:\\n- What are the common patterns / deployment options that are supported?\\n- When I need to do Z, is there a set of accelerators that bootstrap my starting point?\\n- For my use-case how do I know what paved path to choose?\\n:::\\n\\nOutputs would be common blueprints for popular use cases, highlighting technology choices, non-functional characteristics and security. Blueprints should select the building blocks needed to realise the desired architecture, compose them together and provide tooling that allows teams to bootstrap and go. **Blueprints are not pretty pictures - they are evolving software**.\\n\\nE.g.\\n- A Java based internal API\\n- A Python based public API\\n- A .NET based asynchronous service\\n- A public facing static web application\\n\\nThe focus should be on enabling self-service. Iterating on this tooling with teams is critically important; we can easily get stuck into months of development building a CLI tool that no one will use. Make these blueprints accessible, get feedback from your users and iterate.\\n\\nExamples would be (from basic to advanced)\\n- A set of steps on a wiki, illustrating which steps to follow\\n- Repositories with fully functional code (app & infra) that teams can clone, run and modify\\n- A CLI tool that walks them through a wizard, creating repo\'s, infrastructure and pipelines as needed\\n\\n![](paved-path-blueprint-accelerate.png)\\n\\n**Funnels** are decision trees that allow teams to walk through their use case and end up selecting the correct blueprint. E.g. Asking questions around the users or systems that interact with the service being built. These questions will lead to different blueprints: one for client credential grant flow or one for PKCE. Or helping a team decide if their service is public facing and needs to be protected by a public facing WAF directly.\\n\\n![](paved-paths-anatomy-funnel.png)\\n\\n## Culture\\n\\nInterwoven throughout your paved paths will be elements that influence the way your teams work. You may be moving teams towards trunk-based development or separating release and deployment using feature flags. Without guidance as to the why, and how to use the techniques effectively, your paved paths with struggle to gain traction, adoption and success.\\n\\nSimilarly, by shifting functions left, or enabling autonomy, there will be new responsibilities that teams are now accountable for. These need to be articulated, discussed and understood by all involved - you cannot over communicate these cultural changes.\\n\\nSpend time with the teams adopting your paved paths, understand what aspects of their daily processes are affected and help them adjust. It could be as simple as sharing links such as [Ship / Show / Ask](https://martinfowler.com/articles/ship-show-ask.html) to help them decide when to push straight to trunk. Spend time with engineering managers and agile practitioners in your organisation to help them understand these changes as well. Processes will need to be adapted and updated.\\n\\nCharacteristics of a healthy culture, ready for paved paths would be:\\n- A continuous improvement mindset\\n- An organisation sponsoring long-term value, rather than short-term wins\\n- Collaborative, proactive communication and knowledge sharing\\n- Iterative and incremental development\\n- Embracing DevOps practices, see [Culture - Continuous Delivery](https://continuousdelivery.com/implementing/culture/)\\n\\n**Communicate. Listen. Iterate - As a collective.**\\n\\n\\n## Documentation and Onboarding\\n\\nWhen thinking of how to catalogue and share paved paths, the first thing that will most likely jump to mind is [backstage.io](https://backstage.io/) or similar Internal Developer Portal (IDP). We will immediately temper the enthusiasm. Getting an IDP setup and showcasing all your paved paths will take time, time that could be better spent creating more building blocks and blueprints. You will most likely end up with an IDP, but gradually work towards that goal - a shiny portal doesn\'t enable teams to be autonomous or get up and running quickly, but a good set of building blocks, blueprints and a well-written `README.md` will.\\n\\nSplit your documentation into two categories - one for the users of the paved paths (your engineers) and one for contributors to the paved paths. This will allow you to focus on enablement for engineers using the paved paths and separate, deep, technical \\"how this all works\\" style documentation. \\n\\nInside each of these categories, using the [Divio Documentation System](https://documentation.divio.com/) is a great place to start. It provides simple principles to guide how your documents be structured into four functions: tutorials, how-to guides, reference and explanation. Keep the functions distinct from one another and split into the two audience categories. E.g. Under tutorials for your engineers, you would expect to find a tutorial on getting an API into production. Under tutorials for your contributors, you would expect to find a tutorial on adding a new terraform module to your infrastructure building blocks.\\n\\n:::tip \\nThe key thing to keep in mind at all times is the journey that the hikers are taking through your documentation. Put yourself in their shoes, blank out everything you already know, and stare at the woodland with fresh eyes. How well does your documentation hold up? Is it clear where to start? Is it intuitive to navigate? Can your great tooling actually be discovered? If an internal tool drops in the forest, but no one is around to hear it, does it make a sound?\\n:::\\n\\nGetting teams started using your paved paths is critical to their success and should be straight-forward. Teams using your documentation will test it - no documentation will survive first contact with them and this feedback is vital to help you improve. When onboarding teams, make feedback loops on the experience explicit - create mechanisms for them to ask questions, provide feedback and enable you to learn. Make getting help a first class citizen, add `--help`  to all your tools with links to your documentation, create a messaging channel for questions and answers. Questions your users have will highlight gaps in the developer experience as well as places that require more enablement help. Similarly, make giving feedback a first class citizen - don\'t be afraid to poll your users.\\n\\nWith whatever we produce, the vital thing is that we\'re treating our paved paths with the respect they deserve. Just as we have UX (user experience) for our client-facing products, we have DX (developer experience) for our paved paths. Fundamentally, all the same techniques should be used here as we would use for client-facing products. Research, agile methodologies, continuous feedback, continuous improvement. To succeed, we can use techniques we\'re already familiar with, the only difference is that our customer is an engineer!\\n\\nNext, in part 5, we\'ll discuss where Platform Engineering comes into all of this, and whether you *need* it to build paved paths. - read it [here](https://www.rickroche.com/2023/06/paved-paths-series-part-5-the-spectrum/)\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- [The Twelve-Factor App](https://12factor.net/)\\n- [Build your Own Radar | Thoughtworks](https://www.thoughtworks.com/radar/byor)\\n- [What is a Paved Path?.by Matt Stine | CodeX | Medium](https://medium.com/codex/what-is-a-paved-path-b2294463a3a9)\\n- [Create your Paved Roads (hella-secure.com)](https://www.hella-secure.com/post/create-your-paved-roads)\\n- [Paving the Road to Production (infoq.com)](https://www.infoq.com/articles/paving-road-production-coinbase/)\\n- [Designing Golden Paths (redhat.com)](https://cloud.redhat.com/blog/designing-golden-paths)\\n- [How We Use Golden Paths to Solve Fragmentation in Our Software Ecosystem - Spotify Engineering : Spotify Engineering (atspotify.com)](https://engineering.atspotify.com/2020/08/how-we-use-golden-paths-to-solve-fragmentation-in-our-software-ecosystem/)"},{"id":"paved-paths-series-part-2-a-one-pager","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/paved-paths-series-part-2-a-one-pager","editUrl":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen/ctaceygreen.github.io/blog/2023-04-30-paved-paths-02-one-pager/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2023-04-30-paved-paths-02-one-pager/index.md","title":"Paved Paths Series - Part 2 - A One Pager","description":"Part 2 of the series - Your one-pager to demonstrate the value of paved paths","date":"2023-04-30T00:00:00.000Z","formattedDate":"April 30, 2023","tags":[{"label":"paved-path","permalink":"/blog/tags/paved-path"},{"label":"one-pager","permalink":"/blog/tags/one-pager"},{"label":"summary","permalink":"/blog/tags/summary"}],"readingTime":3.315,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Chris Tacey-Green","title":"Engineer, Architect, Human","url":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen","image_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4"},{"name":"Rick Roche","title":"Software Engineer, Solutions Architect, Hiking Enthusiast","url":"https://www.rickroche.com/","image_url":"https://www.rickroche.com/images/avatar.png","imageURL":"https://www.rickroche.com/images/avatar.png"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"paved-paths-series-part-2-a-one-pager","title":"Paved Paths Series - Part 2 - A One Pager","description":"Part 2 of the series - Your one-pager to demonstrate the value of paved paths","authors":[{"name":"Chris Tacey-Green","title":"Engineer, Architect, Human","url":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen","image_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4"},{"name":"Rick Roche","title":"Software Engineer, Solutions Architect, Hiking Enthusiast","url":"https://www.rickroche.com/","image_url":"https://www.rickroche.com/images/avatar.png","imageURL":"https://www.rickroche.com/images/avatar.png"}],"image":"https://christaceygreen.com/2023-04-30-paved-paths-02-one-pager/title-image.png","tags":["paved-path","one-pager","summary"]},"prevItem":{"title":"Paved Paths Series - Part 4 - The Anatomy of Paved Paths","permalink":"/blog/paved-paths-series-part-4-the-anatomy-of-paved-paths"},"nextItem":{"title":"Returning","permalink":"/blog/returning"}},"content":"![title image reading \\"Paved Paths: A one pager\\" with a hiking photo](/2023-04-30-paved-paths-02-one-pager/title-image.png)\\n\\n:::info Paved Path Series\\nThis is Part 2 in a series of posts discussing Paved Paths between [Rick](https://www.rickroche.com/) and I.\\n- [Part 1 - Paved Paths: Let\'s talk about paved paths](https://www.rickroche.com/2023/04/paved-paths-series-part-1-lets-talk-about-paved-paths)\\n- [Part 3 - Paved Paths: Why Paved Paths?](https://www.rickroche.com/2023/05/paved-paths-series-part-3-why-paved-paths)\\n- [Part 4 - Paved Paths: The Anatomy of Paved Paths](https://christaceygreen.com/blog/paved-paths-series-part-4-the-anatomy-of-paved-paths)\\n- [Part 5 - Paved Paths: The Spectrum of Platform Engineering and Paved Paths](https://www.rickroche.com/2023/06/paved-paths-series-part-5-the-spectrum/)\\n:::\\n\\n:::note Why just one page?\\nWe both found ourselves needing to write a one-page summary of paved paths. This would most likely act as a landing page for someone to \'get the gist\' of what these are. Low detail. Imagine the CxO of your company wants to know what these are.\\n:::\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n# What is a paved path?\\nA paved path ensures that a well-trodden route is an easy, quick and safe way of reaching a clearly defined destination.\\n\\n## Hiking\\nImagine you\'re hiking through a woodland. Your destination is \'Chuck\'s Caf\xe9\', 4 miles away. You have a couple of options in this situation:\\n1. Woodland map in hand, follow the predefined path to the caf\xe9.\\n2. Knife in hand, head directly into the bush, confident you have the navigation and athletic abilities to reach the caf\xe9.\\n\\n![image of a path through woodland with two possible routes](./forest_path.png)\\n\\nClearly, #2 is a much more adventurous decision to make. It may result in you learning new skills, or honing your existing ones. It could drive you to solve problems in new, innovative ways. However, it will most likely cause you to reach the caf\xe9 much later than if you\'d followed the predefined path, dishevelled and missing your watch.\\n\\nOption #1 is our paved path. It is predictable, safe, and fast. It will need walkers to make very few decisions(maybe a fork or two), will be smooth to traverse, and protects them from the risks of the deeper woodland (see: Bears). It is also unlikely to yield any opportunities for innovation.\\n\\nThis is the equivalent of our Engineers aiming for their destination of a \'REST API\'. Nothing is stopping them from taking the untrodden path, where they will define patterns from scratch, and solve all of the problems along the way. It takes time though. It\'s also risky; there may well be challenges the team hadn\'t even considered when they started the journey, like securing their API against internal attackers. A paved path takes care of all of this for them, and much like a cookie-cutter, allows them to produce, in this example, APIs of consistent quality in a predictable amount of time.\\n\\n# Does this turn us into a stale assembly line?\\n\\nNo. But it could, if used incorrectly.\\n\\nTo succeed, we need exploitation **and** exploration. \\n\\nIf all of our teams only ever follow a fixed paved path, innovation dies. The path never evolves. The destination never changes. The technical value of the company will slowly diminish, until we wake up and realise none of our customer base owns a fax machine anymore.\\n\\nAt the other extreme, if every team is adventuring into the woodland when 80% of them are trying to reach the same destination, we\'re not exploiting enough. We\'re solving the same problems in different ways, over and over again, instead of focusing on innovating elsewhere.\\n\\nSo... we need both. We need paved paths, which serves the 80% across common use cases. We deliver consistently and with quality. The 20% are in the woodland, exploring new paths, new patterns, new technologies. These new paths may become forks on the paved path, as destinations evolve. If we ensure a balance between exploitation and exploration, paved paths massively empower our engineers and the organisation as a whole, without ever becoming stale.\\n\\nIn part 3 we go into more detail on why organisations need paved paths - read it [here](https://www.rickroche.com/2023/05/paved-paths-series-part-3-why-paved-paths) !\\n\\nFeatured image background by [Austin Ban](https://unsplash.com/@austinban?utm_source=unsplash&utm_medium=referral&utm_content=creditCopyText) on [Unsplash](https://unsplash.com/photos/juHayWuaaoQ?utm_source=unsplash&utm_medium=referral&utm_content=creditCopyText)\\nMap image generated by [Craiyon](https://www.craiyon.com/), updated using [excalidraw](https://github.com/excalidraw/excalidraw)"},{"id":"returning","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/returning","editUrl":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen/ctaceygreen.github.io/blog/2023-04-26-returning/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2023-04-26-returning/index.md","title":"Returning","description":"So I disappeared from blogging for...a year and a half.","date":"2023-04-26T00:00:00.000Z","formattedDate":"April 26, 2023","tags":[{"label":"returning","permalink":"/blog/tags/returning"}],"readingTime":0.735,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Chris Tacey-Green","title":"Engineer, Architect, Human","url":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"returning","title":"Returning","author":"Chris Tacey-Green","author_title":"Engineer, Architect, Human","author_url":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen","author_image_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4","tags":["returning"]},"prevItem":{"title":"Paved Paths Series - Part 2 - A One Pager","permalink":"/blog/paved-paths-series-part-2-a-one-pager"},"nextItem":{"title":"How do I check the health of my Azure Consumption Functions when swapping Deployment Slots?","permalink":"/blog/azure-consumption-functions-healthcheck-with-deployment-slots"}},"content":"So I disappeared from blogging for...a year and a half.\\n\\nI apologise. I was focusing my energy on a very important deployment - my daughter! And if I\'m honest, on balance, my daughter probably wins out in importance versus this blog.\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\nBut my daughter is now an incredibly cute, happy and genuinely funny 1 year old. I reckon we\'re at a point where I can find spare minutes in my day to get back into blogging. This isn\'t just a random thought though, I have plans! I\'m currently working on a series of posts, alongside an epic human, on a topic I find extremely interesting. We also happen to believe it\'s vitally important given the direction the industry is heading.\\n\\nSo not only will I be back, but I\'ll be back with some regular posts! It\'s an Easter miracle.\\n\\nOK, that\'s all. See you very soon..."},{"id":"azure-consumption-functions-healthcheck-with-deployment-slots","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/azure-consumption-functions-healthcheck-with-deployment-slots","editUrl":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen/ctaceygreen.github.io/blog/2021-08-26-azure-consumption-functions-healthcheck-with-deployment-slots/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2021-08-26-azure-consumption-functions-healthcheck-with-deployment-slots/index.md","title":"How do I check the health of my Azure Consumption Functions when swapping Deployment Slots?","description":"Setting the scene","date":"2021-08-26T00:00:00.000Z","formattedDate":"August 26, 2021","tags":[{"label":"deployment-slots","permalink":"/blog/tags/deployment-slots"},{"label":"healthcheck","permalink":"/blog/tags/healthcheck"},{"label":"consumption","permalink":"/blog/tags/consumption"},{"label":"function","permalink":"/blog/tags/function"},{"label":"azure","permalink":"/blog/tags/azure"}],"readingTime":3.425,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Chris Tacey-Green","title":"Engineer, Architect, Human","url":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"azure-consumption-functions-healthcheck-with-deployment-slots","title":"How do I check the health of my Azure Consumption Functions when swapping Deployment Slots?","author":"Chris Tacey-Green","author_title":"Engineer, Architect, Human","author_url":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen","author_image_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4","tags":["deployment-slots","healthcheck","consumption","function","azure"]},"prevItem":{"title":"Returning","permalink":"/blog/returning"},"nextItem":{"title":"Welcome","permalink":"/blog/welcome"}},"content":"## Setting the scene\\n\\nOkay that title has quite a few elements to it, so let\'s break down exactly what we\'re trying to do here...\\n\\nOur situation:\\n* An Azure Function running on a consumption plan\\n* [Deployment slots](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/deploy-staging-slots) configured within our IaC (a guide on these could be a separate post)\\n* An ADO pipeline that deploys our IaC, code and then swaps our staging and production slot\\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\n## So what\'s the problem here?\\n\\nOur problem is that with the current setup, swapping slots on a consumption Function assumes healthiness based on loading the root URL of your Function App. This may be suitable for simple Functions, but as soon as your Function has downstream dependencies (such as a reliance on a DB, queue or other API), our requirements become more complicated. Ideally, our deployment process would look something like :\\n* Deploy infrastructure\\n* Deploy code to staging slot\\n* Check downstream dependencies are correctly configured and accessible to staging slot\\n* Swap staging and production slot\\n\\nHopefully you can see now that our root URL is letting us down slightly.\\n\\nThis is where we can turn to health-checks. In many services (App Service, Kubernetes etc), zero-downtime deployments utilise health probes, which hit a predefined HTTP endpoint to determine that your app is ready and healthy. An experienced blogger and all-round smart guy named John Reilly has already created a similar post [here](https://blog.johnnyreilly.com/2021/02/11/azure-app-service-health-checks-and-zero-downtime-deployments) to explain how we can achieve this for Azure App Service. This process can be followed if you\'re running your Functions on an App Service plan. If only this worked for consumption Functions too, my post would be completely unnecessary!\\nUnfortunately, it doesn\'t. The `healthcheckPath` used in App Service to facilitate this functionality is not supported on the consumption plan. Annoying, but we still have a solution!\\n\\n## The solution\\n\\nThere are some sneaky appsettings that we can set on our Function. These configure which endpoint is used during slot warmup to check your slot is healthy. The appsettings are as follows:\\n\\n```json\\n\\"appsettings\\" : [\\n    {\\n        \\"name\\": \\"WEBSITE_WARMUP_PATH\\",\\n        \\"value\\": \\"[parameters(\'healthcheckPath\')]\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n        \\"name\\": \\"WEBSITE_SWAP_WARMUP_PING_PATH\\",\\n        \\"value\\": \\"[parameters(\'healthcheckPath\')]\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n        \\"name\\": \\"WEBSITE_SWAP_WARMUP_PING_STATUSES\\",\\n        \\"value\\": \\"200\\"\\n    }\\n    ...\\n]\\n```\\n\\n* `WEBSITE_WARMUP_PATH` - Used in a few different areas of Azure. The reason we need it is because we have authentication enabled across all endpoints of our Function. Unlike the healthcheckPath functionality of app-service, the Function slot warmup process does not run \'behind\' the auth/ip restriction layer. Therefore, this appsetting specifies that the authentication layer should ignore this path and allow anonymous calls. This should be considered carefully with your use-case, ensuring that you\'re not opening yourself up to DOS attacks or returning sensitive information that can be used as a security hole.\\n* `WEBSITE_SWAP_WARMUP_PING_PATH` - The path to your healthcheck/ping endpoint on your Function. e.g. \'/api/healthcheck\'\\n* `WEBSITE_SWAP_WARMUP_PING_STATUSES` - The statuses that you will accept as a successful response from your healthcheck endpoint. Without this, Azure will consider some redirect status codes as successful responses, so in our example we\'re making this much more specific with just a \'200\' considered successful.\\n\\nAfter adding this into your ARM template for the Function `Microsoft.Web/Sites` and it\'s associated slot, you\'re good to go! The final step is to ensure that your ADO pipeline is running the task below to swap slots on your Function:\\n\\n```yml\\n- task: AzureAppServiceManager@0\\n  displayName: \'Swap Staging Slot with Production\'\\n  inputs:\\n    action: \'Swap Slots\'\\n    azureSubscription: \'$(azureSubscription)\'\\n    resourceGroupName: $(resourceGroupName)\\n    webAppName: $(functionName)\\n    SourceSlot: \'stage\'\\n```\\n\\nRunning this ADO pipeline step will now check your `WEBSITE_SWAP_WARMUP_PING_PATH` is returning the expected status before swapping your slot into production. This should greatly reduce the likelihood of a consumption Function being deployed to production with misconfigured dependencies (e.g. invalid connection-string). You could easily implement this pattern for any consumption Functions within your estate, even non-http-triggers, all you\'d need to do is ensure that an associated healthcheck http-trigger Function was deployed alongside it.\\n\\nThat should do us for this one! If anything isn\'t clear, get in touch and I\'d be happy to provide more reproducable examples of the entire ARM template and pipeline steps.\\n\\nUntil next time..."},{"id":"welcome","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/welcome","editUrl":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen/ctaceygreen.github.io/blog/2021-08-1-welcome/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2021-08-1-welcome/index.md","title":"Welcome","description":"I figured the first post couldn\'t be a heavy technical braindump. That would be rude.","date":"2021-08-01T00:00:00.000Z","formattedDate":"August 1, 2021","tags":[{"label":"welcome","permalink":"/blog/tags/welcome"}],"readingTime":0.985,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Chris Tacey-Green","title":"Engineer, Architect, Human","url":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"welcome","title":"Welcome","author":"Chris Tacey-Green","author_title":"Engineer, Architect, Human","author_url":"https://github.com/ctaceygreen","author_image_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11404995?v=4","tags":["welcome"]},"prevItem":{"title":"How do I check the health of my Azure Consumption Functions when swapping Deployment Slots?","permalink":"/blog/azure-consumption-functions-healthcheck-with-deployment-slots"}},"content":"I figured the first post couldn\'t be a heavy technical braindump. That would be rude. \\n\\n\x3c!--truncate--\x3e\\n\\nInstead, here\'s a much more bite-size and welcoming \'Welcome\' post, to break the blog in.\\nSince I\'m British, this is where I\'d naturally talk about the weather to break the ice. So.. it\'s the middle of Summer in the UK, and therefore has been wet, windy and grey today.\\n\\nGood.\\n\\nWith that out of the way, here\'s a quick summary of what topics to expect from the first bunch of posts:\\n* Approaching deployment in Azure Devops\\n* Monitoring in Azure\\n* Alerting in Azure\\n* Logging in Azure (Functions and App Service)\\n* Securing an application in Azure App Service\\n* Azure integration technologies\\n* Why services in Azure want to be event-driven\\n* Building an event-driven solution in Azure\\n* Things you\'ll need in your Azure Function code\\n* Why templates are your friend\\n* Integration in a Hybrid cloud model\\n\\nVery Azure-heavy, I realise. But those who have poked me to create this blog are hoping for some Azure guidance, so this is where I\'ll start my journey into blogging.\\n\\nI better get writing my first post then..."}]}')}}]);